          "en": "A short story"
        },
        "text": {
          "en": "Meera drew the Moon's shape every evening for a month.\n\"It goes from thin to full and back,\" she said."
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "explain",
        "type": "explanation",
        "provenance": "original",
        "keepEnglishTerms": [
          "phase",
          "tide",
          "gravity"
        ],
        "text": {
          "en": "The Moon goes round the Earth in about 29.5 days. We see different amounts of its lit half, called phases, from new moon to full moon.\nThe Moon's gravity pulls the sea and makes tides, the rise and fall of sea level. High tide comes about twice a day.\nSpring tides are highest at new and full moon, when the Sun, Moon and Earth line up."
        }
